WEBVTT

00:00:20.646 --> 00:00:22.773
[David Attenborough] <i>Just 50 years ago,</i>

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.276
<i>we finally ventured to the moon.</i>

00:00:40.332 --> 00:00:45.837
<i>For the very first time,</i>
<i>we looked back at our own planet.</i>

00:00:53.345 --> 00:00:58.684
<i>Since then, the human population</i>
<i>has more than doubled.</i>

00:01:04.189 --> 00:01:08.652
<i>This series will celebrate</i>
<i>the natural wonders that remain,</i>

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.281
<i>and reveal what we must preserve</i>

00:01:12.656 --> 00:01:16.827
<i>to ensure people and nature thrive.</i>

00:01:41.018 --> 00:01:46.565
<i>The polar regions of our planet</i>
<i>may seem beyond the reach of most of us,</i>

00:01:52.821 --> 00:01:55.532
<i>but they are not beyond our influence.</i>

00:02:00.579 --> 00:02:05.459
<i>We, unintentionally,</i>
<i>are changing these frozen worlds,</i>

00:02:08.879 --> 00:02:11.965
<i>and these changes</i>
<i>will not just affect the poles</i>

00:02:12.841 --> 00:02:14.426
<i>but the whole planet.</i>

00:02:44.039 --> 00:02:45.082
<i>Antarctica.</i>

00:02:48.293 --> 00:02:52.464
<i>The southern end of our planet,</i>
<i>and the coldest place on Earth.</i>

00:02:55.926 --> 00:02:59.388
<i>It has been frozen for 30 million years.</i>

00:03:01.723 --> 00:03:05.977
<i>In its center, the covering of ice</i>
<i>is over four kilometers thick.</i>

00:03:09.564 --> 00:03:12.526
<i>Entire mountain ranges</i>
<i>are buried beneath it.</i>

00:03:15.946 --> 00:03:19.032
<i>Here, it is so cold that each winter</i>

00:03:19.116 --> 00:03:23.161
<i>19 million square kilometers of ocean</i>
<i>freezes,</i>

00:03:23.578 --> 00:03:26.123
<i>more than doubling</i>
<i>the size of the ice cap.</i>

00:03:30.168 --> 00:03:33.046
<i>But when, in spring, the sea ice melts,</i>

00:03:33.463 --> 00:03:37.551
<i>life returns once again</i>
<i>to the continent's shores.</i>

00:03:45.142 --> 00:03:48.478
<i>The peninsula that stretches north</i>
<i>towards South America</i>

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:52.482
<i>is the first part of it</i>
<i>to be released from winter's grip.</i>

00:03:59.948 --> 00:04:01.616
<i>As the sea ice breaks up,</i>

00:04:03.577 --> 00:04:05.328
<i>life returns.</i>

00:04:25.849 --> 00:04:26.850
[gulls cawing]

00:04:30.645 --> 00:04:33.899
There is a <i>greater variety</i>
<i>of living creatures here</i>

00:04:34.191 --> 00:04:36.818
<i>than anywhere else in Antarctica.</i>

00:04:54.336 --> 00:04:58.340
<i>Gentoo penguins</i>
<i>spend most of their lives at sea,</i>

00:05:00.467 --> 00:05:04.971
<i>but now, in spring,</i>
<i>they have to come ashore to breed.</i>

00:05:06.181 --> 00:05:09.100
<i>They're the fastest of all penguins</i>
<i>in water,</i>

00:05:11.102 --> 00:05:15.148
<i>but on land,</i>
<i>life takes on a slower pace.</i>

00:05:26.743 --> 00:05:30.413
<i>It's an uphill struggle</i>
<i>to reach their nesting grounds...</i>

00:05:32.415 --> 00:05:34.709
[penguins honking]

00:05:35.752 --> 00:05:37.671
<i>...but penguins never give up.</i>

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:48.181
<i>The paths they follow,</i>
<i>carved by thousands of tiny footsteps,</i>

00:05:48.431 --> 00:05:51.017
<i>lead to Antarctica's rarest commodity.</i>

00:05:56.773 --> 00:05:57.774
<i>Bare rock.</i>

00:06:04.656 --> 00:06:08.159
<i>Less than one percent of Antarctica</i>
<i>is ice-free,</i>

00:06:09.327 --> 00:06:10.912
<i>and these rocky patches</i>

00:06:11.037 --> 00:06:14.541
<i>are the only places</i>
<i>where gentoos can lay their eggs.</i>

00:06:20.005 --> 00:06:22.132
<i>It's been a 30-minute struggle,</i>

00:06:26.219 --> 00:06:29.931
<i>but he's almost at the top,</i>
<i>where his mate is waiting for him.</i>

00:06:39.774 --> 00:06:42.694
[honking]

00:06:46.031 --> 00:06:48.992
<i>He presents her with a stone,</i>

00:06:49.743 --> 00:06:53.663
<i>a gift to improve the nest</i>
<i>and so win her favor.</i>

00:06:55.165 --> 00:06:57.083
[both honking]

00:07:02.589 --> 00:07:04.716
<i>He's back just in time.</i>

00:07:08.595 --> 00:07:10.931
<i>There's another mouth to feed.</i>

00:07:22.859 --> 00:07:23.985
[chirrups]

00:07:26.863 --> 00:07:31.117
<i>Gentoo penguins used to be rare</i>
<i>in this part of Antarctica,</i>

00:07:31.201 --> 00:07:36.247
<i>but now, as temperatures rise,</i>
<i>their numbers here are increasing.</i>

00:07:44.381 --> 00:07:48.385
<i>Sea ice may appear to be flat</i>
<i>and lifeless,</i>

00:07:50.679 --> 00:07:54.641
<i>but beneath it there is another world.</i>

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:04.901
<i>As the ice melts,</i>
<i>the light filters through,</i>

00:08:05.026 --> 00:08:09.030
<i>and algae, that have been trapped</i>
<i>within the ice all winter, flourish.</i>

00:08:11.866 --> 00:08:14.119
<i>This upside-down world</i>

00:08:14.244 --> 00:08:17.330
<i>is the polar equivalent</i>
<i>of the great grasslands.</i>

00:08:20.709 --> 00:08:24.462
<i>The ice is the soil</i>
<i>upon which plant life grows,</i>

00:08:25.630 --> 00:08:28.675
<i>and the herds of grazers soon arrive.</i>

00:08:33.471 --> 00:08:34.639
<i>Antarctic krill.</i>

00:08:37.934 --> 00:08:41.479
<i>Trillions of them swarm</i>
<i>in the waters around Antarctica.</i>

00:08:42.564 --> 00:08:47.569
<i>Protected by the ice above,</i>
<i>they graze on algae whilst the sun shines.</i>

00:08:54.576 --> 00:08:56.953
<i>But the good times don't last long.</i>

00:08:59.539 --> 00:09:03.501
<i>Once all the ice has melted,</i>
<i>the krill lose their protection,</i>

00:09:09.215 --> 00:09:10.842
<i>and predators appear.</i>

00:09:21.561 --> 00:09:24.481
<i>Penguins may have lost</i>
<i>their ability to fly,</i>

00:09:24.814 --> 00:09:28.068
<i>but they still form flocks,</i>
<i>as so many birds do.</i>

00:09:40.455 --> 00:09:41.831
<i>The longer they dive,</i>

00:09:41.915 --> 00:09:44.751
<i>the longer it takes them</i>
<i>to catch their breath.</i>

00:09:51.508 --> 00:09:57.013
<i>Ruffling their feathers traps air,</i>
<i>which makes their bodies more streamlined.</i>

00:10:00.183 --> 00:10:01.476
<i>One last breath...</i>

00:10:04.646 --> 00:10:06.898
<i>and the whole group dives down together.</i>

00:10:14.906 --> 00:10:19.828
<i>The gentoos follow the krill</i>
<i>down to depths of over 200 meters.</i>

00:10:36.344 --> 00:10:39.055
<i>What a flock can catch in one dive,</i>

00:10:39.806 --> 00:10:43.184
<i>a giant traps in a single mouthful.</i>

00:11:01.703 --> 00:11:03.496
<i>Humpback whales from the tropics</i>

00:11:03.746 --> 00:11:07.250
<i>have traveled</i>
<i>over 8,000 kilometers to get here.</i>

00:11:11.254 --> 00:11:14.007
<i>This one chooses to feed alone.</i>

00:11:26.811 --> 00:11:29.731
<i>This technique, part lunge, part pounce,</i>

00:11:30.315 --> 00:11:33.359
<i>only works when the krill</i>
<i>are close to the surface.</i>

00:11:35.987 --> 00:11:40.533
<i>As more humpbacks arrive,</i>
<i>they begin to work in teams.</i>

00:11:51.794 --> 00:11:53.254
<i>By lunging together,</i>

00:11:53.338 --> 00:11:58.509
<i>the overspill from one huge mouth</i>
<i>is not lost but collected by another.</i>

00:12:39.133 --> 00:12:44.430
<i>Some humpbacks regularly work</i>
<i>in an even more coordinated way.</i>

00:13:01.281 --> 00:13:04.784
<i>They do so</i>
<i>when krill has descended to some depth.</i>

00:13:14.919 --> 00:13:19.007
<i>Once in position,</i>
<i>they blast air from their blowholes.</i>

00:13:28.850 --> 00:13:29.976
<i>Working together,</i>

00:13:30.059 --> 00:13:33.396
<i>they create a curtain of bubbles</i>
<i>around their prey.</i>

00:13:42.071 --> 00:13:46.868
<i>As the spiraling net tightens,</i>
<i>the krill are driven closer together,</i>

00:13:49.454 --> 00:13:53.082
<i>and then the whales lunge upwards</i>
<i>and collect them.</i>

00:14:16.439 --> 00:14:19.484
<i>Almost all humpback whales</i>
<i>in the southern hemisphere</i>

00:14:19.567 --> 00:14:22.820
<i>come to the Antarctic to feed on krill.</i>

00:14:29.702 --> 00:14:34.123
<i>Since the ban on commercial whaling,</i>
<i>their numbers have recovered dramatically,</i>

00:14:35.583 --> 00:14:38.461
<i>but their food supply is now under threat.</i>

00:14:42.256 --> 00:14:44.133
<i>In the last 50 years,</i>

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:47.678
<i>with warming temperatures</i>
<i>and disappearing sea ice,</i>

00:14:48.179 --> 00:14:50.973
<i>krill stocks</i>
<i>in this part of the Southern Ocean</i>

00:14:51.057 --> 00:14:53.059
<i>have more than halved.</i>

00:15:00.858 --> 00:15:05.405
<i>Other sea hunters come here</i>
<i>seeking bigger prey than krill.</i>

00:15:16.124 --> 00:15:18.126
<i>Antarctica's top predators.</i>

00:15:20.878 --> 00:15:23.965
<i>Orca, killer whales.</i>

00:15:26.634 --> 00:15:31.180
<i>Over half of the world's orca</i>
<i>patrol these polar seas.</i>

00:15:36.853 --> 00:15:42.442
<i>This particular pod</i>
<i>specializes in hunting penguins.</i>

00:15:53.119 --> 00:15:56.622
<i>They shift into stealth mode.</i>

00:16:03.754 --> 00:16:09.051
<i>Hunting without making a sound,</i>
<i>so that their prey won't hear them coming.</i>

00:16:37.079 --> 00:16:40.541
<i>A penguin can outmaneuver</i>
<i>a single killer whale,</i>

00:16:41.542 --> 00:16:43.711
<i>but it can seldom escape a pod.</i>

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:13.324
<i>This hunt may seem cruel,</i>

00:17:14.742 --> 00:17:19.288
<i>but important life skills</i>
<i>are being passed down the generations.</i>

00:17:41.561 --> 00:17:43.729
<i>From killer whales to krill,</i>

00:17:44.188 --> 00:17:49.569
<i>all life here ultimately depends</i>
<i>upon Antarctica's sea ice.</i>

00:18:09.130 --> 00:18:13.593
<i>The seas that surround Antarctica</i>
<i>are the roughest on our planet.</i>

00:18:23.978 --> 00:18:27.857
<i>These churning waters</i>
<i>draw up nutrients from the deep</i>

00:18:28.024 --> 00:18:33.863
<i>and create vast, rich fishing grounds</i>
<i>for nomads like the wandering albatross.</i>

00:18:51.547 --> 00:18:55.760
<i>Beneath the waves,</i>
<i>currents are flowing away from Antarctica,</i>

00:18:56.052 --> 00:19:00.348
<i>transporting nutrients across the planet,</i>
<i>fertilizing the oceans</i>

00:19:00.723 --> 00:19:03.225
<i>and helping to regulate</i>
<i>global temperatures.</i>

00:19:30.002 --> 00:19:32.880
<i>The island of South Georgia.</i>

00:19:37.259 --> 00:19:38.678
<i>It's far enough north</i>

00:19:38.844 --> 00:19:42.348
<i>to be beyond the reach of the sea ice</i>
<i>even in midwinter,</i>

00:19:43.557 --> 00:19:46.102
<i>and many animals live here</i>
<i>the year round.</i>

00:20:04.453 --> 00:20:09.834
<i>This wandering albatross chick</i>
<i>has been sitting on its nest all winter,</i>

00:20:11.085 --> 00:20:15.381
<i>and is entirely dependent</i>
<i>on its parents returning with food.</i>

00:20:17.550 --> 00:20:22.346
<i>It may have to wait a few days</i>
<i>between meals, or even a couple of weeks,</i>

00:20:23.013 --> 00:20:26.267
<i>but at least</i>
<i>it's not the only one who's waiting.</i>

00:20:30.187 --> 00:20:34.567
<i>Albatross chicks need to spend</i>
<i>a whole year growing</i>

00:20:34.817 --> 00:20:37.278
<i>before they're ready</i>
<i>to take their first flight.</i>

00:20:44.910 --> 00:20:50.332
<i>And then they spend several years at sea</i>
<i>before they return to land.</i>

00:20:55.921 --> 00:20:58.007
[honking]

00:21:05.097 --> 00:21:09.643
<i>Coming in to land </i>
<i>on three-meter wings takes some practice.</i>

00:21:12.313 --> 00:21:15.274
<i>Albatross don't have reverse thrusters.</i>

00:21:21.322 --> 00:21:23.073
[chirping]

00:21:35.711 --> 00:21:38.589
<i>The chick </i>
<i>is this female's first.</i>

00:21:42.885 --> 00:21:47.431
<i>She's been raising it for six months,</i>
<i>and has another six to go.</i>

00:21:51.685 --> 00:21:54.355
<i>Her chick is smaller than it should be,</i>

00:21:54.855 --> 00:21:58.317
<i>a sign that her mate</i>
<i>has not been returning with food.</i>

00:22:00.277 --> 00:22:03.197
<i>He may well have been</i>
<i>one of the many that die,</i>

00:22:03.322 --> 00:22:06.158
<i>entangled on longline fishing lines.</i>

00:22:11.956 --> 00:22:16.502
<i>South Georgia is an ideal place</i>
<i>for wanderers to raise their young,</i>

00:22:17.878 --> 00:22:21.131
<i>but now fewer adults</i>
<i>are returning here to nest,</i>

00:22:21.632 --> 00:22:22.883
<i>and in recent years,</i>

00:22:22.967 --> 00:22:27.054
<i>the breeding population</i>
<i>has declined by 40 percent.</i>

00:22:34.144 --> 00:22:38.816
<i>For now, waiting on its nest,</i>
<i>the chick is safe.</i>

00:22:39.817 --> 00:22:42.653
<i>There are no land predators here.</i>

00:22:48.450 --> 00:22:52.204
<i>But the same cannot be said</i>
<i>for South Georgia's seas.</i>

00:22:55.624 --> 00:22:59.670
<i>Giant kelp flourishes</i>
<i>in these ice-free waters.</i>

00:23:06.218 --> 00:23:09.471
<i>The perfect place for an ambush.</i>

00:23:17.980 --> 00:23:22.776
<i>Leopard seals overwinter here</i>
<i>to escape Antarctica's frozen ocean.</i>

00:23:25.738 --> 00:23:27.990
<i>They should have returned south by now,</i>

00:23:30.367 --> 00:23:32.536
<i>but this year they've stayed longer.</i>

00:23:39.001 --> 00:23:42.129
<i>And they've delayed their departure</i>
<i>for good reason.</i>

00:23:50.554 --> 00:23:52.431
<i>There are king penguins here.</i>

00:23:58.020 --> 00:24:00.147
<i>Each is a substantial meal,</i>

00:24:01.815 --> 00:24:02.816
<i>if you can catch it.</i>

00:24:11.158 --> 00:24:12.743
<i>Returning to feed their chicks,</i>

00:24:12.993 --> 00:24:16.205
<i>the kings have no choice</i>
<i>but to run the gauntlet.</i>

00:24:24.254 --> 00:24:26.423
<i>Kings rarely porpoise like this.</i>

00:24:28.550 --> 00:24:32.805
<i>It uses more energy,</i>
<i>but it's faster than traveling underwater.</i>

00:25:15.222 --> 00:25:19.143
<i>It's hard to select a moving target</i>
<i>in a panicking crowd,</i>

00:25:25.399 --> 00:25:28.444
<i>but if there are stragglers,</i>
<i>the odds change.</i>

00:25:37.286 --> 00:25:40.831
<i>Razor-sharp teeth versus a stabbing beak.</i>

00:26:12.613 --> 00:26:13.655
<i>It's stalemate.</i>

00:26:14.990 --> 00:26:17.659
<i>Both are exhausted after the chase.</i>

00:26:27.753 --> 00:26:31.590
<i>The hunter decides that the meal</i>
<i>is not worth the fight.</i>

00:26:34.093 --> 00:26:35.469
[cawing]

00:26:41.725 --> 00:26:44.561
<i>For the penguin,</i>
<i>it was too close for comfort,</i>

00:26:44.853 --> 00:26:49.358
<i>but that was just the first hurdle</i>
<i>in getting back to feed its chick.</i>

00:26:53.612 --> 00:26:54.613
[cawing]

00:26:54.738 --> 00:26:57.366
<i>It isn't going to be </i>
<i>a walk in the park.</i>

00:27:02.079 --> 00:27:03.330
[elephant seals snoring]

00:27:25.602 --> 00:27:28.772
<i>Luckily, penguin is not on their menu.</i>

00:27:35.320 --> 00:27:38.782
<i>Still, it's best</i>
<i>to let sleeping giants lie.</i>

00:27:45.414 --> 00:27:46.498
[penguin honks]

00:27:52.421 --> 00:27:55.757
<i>Elephant seal females </i>
<i>don't take kindly</i>

00:27:55.841 --> 00:27:57.801
<i>to being disturbed from their slumber,</i>

00:27:59.386 --> 00:28:03.307
<i>but it's the four-tonne males</i>
<i>you need to look out for.</i>

00:28:03.849 --> 00:28:05.851
[rasping grunting]

00:28:14.151 --> 00:28:17.321
<i>They are fighting</i>
<i>for access to the females.</i>

00:28:25.454 --> 00:28:30.709
<i>Getting bulldozed under angry blubber</i>
<i>is to be avoided at all costs.</i>

00:28:41.678 --> 00:28:42.512
<i>An escape route.</i>

00:28:48.060 --> 00:28:49.144
<i>Time to run for it.</i>

00:29:01.573 --> 00:29:03.992
<i>At last, he's reached the nursery.</i>

00:29:06.411 --> 00:29:08.830
<i>Now comes his final challenge.</i>

00:29:11.124 --> 00:29:14.836
<i>Finding his chick</i>
<i>in the crowd of half a million.</i>

00:29:19.216 --> 00:29:20.384
<i>He calls...</i>

00:29:21.009 --> 00:29:22.761
[cawing]

00:29:29.518 --> 00:29:32.729
<i>...and listens </i>
<i>for a recognizable reply.</i>

00:29:32.813 --> 00:29:34.815
[chirping]

00:29:41.655 --> 00:29:44.366
<i>His chick could be</i>
<i>another mile or two away,</i>

00:29:45.033 --> 00:29:46.368
<i>at the back of the colony.</i>

00:29:50.831 --> 00:29:52.624
<i>But that's the trouble with chicks.</i>

00:29:53.208 --> 00:29:54.918
<i>They're never where you left them.</i>

00:29:57.296 --> 00:29:59.548
<i>Astonishingly, in all this racket,</i>

00:29:59.673 --> 00:30:03.552
<i>a parent and its chick</i>
<i>can recognize each other's calls.</i>

00:30:03.969 --> 00:30:05.512
[chirping]

00:30:13.603 --> 00:30:17.065
<i>The things parents go through</i>
<i>to deliver a meal.</i>

00:30:20.360 --> 00:30:21.695
<i>If only they knew.</i>

00:30:29.286 --> 00:30:31.079
<i>For such a small island,</i>

00:30:31.246 --> 00:30:35.167
<i>South Georgia supports</i>
<i>an extraordinary quantity of life.</i>

00:30:40.005 --> 00:30:43.175
<i>All these creatures</i>
<i>ultimately depend on the krill</i>

00:30:43.508 --> 00:30:46.845
<i>that develops under the sea ice</i>
<i>around the Antarctic peninsula.</i>

00:30:49.890 --> 00:30:51.391
<i>With sea ice disappearing,</i>

00:30:51.892 --> 00:30:55.145
<i>so South Georgia's future</i>
<i>is far from certain.</i>

00:31:11.203 --> 00:31:13.705
<i>At the other end of the planet,</i>
<i>in the north,</i>

00:31:14.414 --> 00:31:18.877
<i>the effects of climate change</i>
<i>are being felt even more intensely.</i>

00:31:21.046 --> 00:31:24.091
<i>The Arctic is not a continent</i>
<i>surrounded by sea</i>

00:31:25.425 --> 00:31:28.261
<i>but a frozen ocean ringed by land.</i>

00:31:32.307 --> 00:31:37.229
<i>Here, in spring, the sea ice</i>
<i>is still at its maximum extent.</i>

00:31:42.734 --> 00:31:48.949
<i>It's a vast, firm hunting ground</i>
<i>for the planet's largest land carnivore.</i>

00:32:00.669 --> 00:32:04.089
<i>A male polar bear</i>
<i>in search of his next meal</i>

00:32:04.506 --> 00:32:08.552
<i>on the frozen fjords of Svalbard</i>
<i>in the high Arctic.</i>

00:32:13.140 --> 00:32:16.893
<i>He's looking for seals,</i>
<i>which make up the bulk of his diet.</i>

00:32:24.526 --> 00:32:28.488
<i>He must eat two-thirds</i>
<i>of the food he needs for the whole year</i>

00:32:28.780 --> 00:32:33.118
<i>before the ice melts</i>
<i>and his hunting platform disappears.</i>

00:33:06.735 --> 00:33:11.740
<i>The ringed seal mother had no choice</i>
<i>but to leave her pup alone on the surface.</i>

00:33:22.417 --> 00:33:26.880
<i>In the past, she would have built a den</i>
<i>inside a ridge on the ice,</i>

00:33:28.590 --> 00:33:32.427
<i>but now the sea freezes so late</i>
<i>that the ice is flat,</i>

00:33:32.928 --> 00:33:36.389
<i>and she had to leave her pup</i>
<i>out in the open.</i>

00:33:47.359 --> 00:33:51.238
<i>These changes in the ice</i>
<i>may benefit the bears for now,</i>

00:33:51.780 --> 00:33:53.448
<i>but in the long term,</i>

00:33:53.532 --> 00:33:56.910
<i>a crash in their most important prey</i>
<i>will follow.</i>

00:34:01.039 --> 00:34:05.627
<i>This new, flatter ice</i>
<i>makes life difficult for the bears, too.</i>

00:34:06.503 --> 00:34:10.423
<i>There's nowhere for them to hide</i>
<i>in order to creep up on a seal.</i>

00:34:15.136 --> 00:34:20.141
<i>And for a mother with a young cub in tow,</i>
<i>being stealthy isn't easy.</i>

00:34:36.324 --> 00:34:39.536
<i>The cub doesn't yet appreciate</i>
<i>the need for care</i>

00:34:41.288 --> 00:34:43.206
<i>when sneaking up on a seal.</i>

00:34:45.917 --> 00:34:48.128
<i>With fewer seals hiding in dens,</i>

00:34:48.336 --> 00:34:51.756
<i>learning this pounding technique,</i>
<i>once so useful,</i>

00:34:52.841 --> 00:34:54.426
<i>is now almost pointless.</i>

00:35:06.229 --> 00:35:10.859
<i>Still, at least he's discovered</i>
<i>what a seal hole looks like..</i>

00:35:22.370 --> 00:35:26.916
<i>His mother knows that overcast conditions</i>
<i>are good for hunting,</i>

00:35:28.543 --> 00:35:30.837
<i>and he knows when she's serious.</i>

00:35:39.304 --> 00:35:42.223
<i>Adult seals make a much better meal.</i>

00:35:54.027 --> 00:35:57.238
<i>Her cub carefully treads in her footsteps,</i>

00:35:59.574 --> 00:36:02.285
<i>and takes care not to break her outline.</i>

00:36:06.623 --> 00:36:08.792
<i>Neither must put a foot wrong.</i>

00:36:15.757 --> 00:36:18.176
<i>They must move as one.</i>

00:36:44.577 --> 00:36:47.539
<i>Most seal hunts end in failure.</i>

00:36:55.171 --> 00:36:57.215
<i>The bears must keep searching.</i>

00:37:01.344 --> 00:37:03.555
<i>Her cub wanders off,</i>

00:37:06.182 --> 00:37:07.851
<i>and gets another chance.</i>

00:37:18.611 --> 00:37:21.448
<i>A seal pup alone on the ice.</i>

00:37:26.327 --> 00:37:29.330
[seal pup whimpering]

00:37:32.167 --> 00:37:36.755
<i>Now it's not stealth </i>
<i>that is needed, but confidence.</i>

00:37:41.843 --> 00:37:43.887
<i>There's nothing the mother can do,</i>

00:37:46.473 --> 00:37:50.560
<i>except hope that the pup makes it</i>
<i>back to her hole in time.</i>

00:37:51.811 --> 00:37:52.645
[whimpers]

00:37:58.151 --> 00:38:00.987
<i>The inexperienced young hunter </i>
<i>hesitates.</i>

00:38:11.331 --> 00:38:12.874
<i>Its mother knows better.</i>

00:38:36.940 --> 00:38:38.942
<i>Safe under the ice.</i>

00:38:44.948 --> 00:38:50.537
<i>Polar bears are adaptable predators,</i>
<i>but they rely on sea ice.</i>

00:38:52.455 --> 00:38:56.125
<i>If that disappears, so will the bears.</i>

00:39:10.223 --> 00:39:13.768
<i>Summer at the ice edge</i>
<i>in the Canadian Arctic,</i>

00:39:14.352 --> 00:39:18.189
<i>and the annual retreat of sea ice</i>
<i>is well underway.</i>

00:39:27.782 --> 00:39:30.702
<i>As it melts, nomads arrive.</i>

00:39:50.305 --> 00:39:54.434
<i>Narwhal, the origin of the unicorn legend.</i>

00:39:55.268 --> 00:40:01.190
<i>Their three-meter tusk is, in fact,</i>
<i>no more than an overgrown canine tooth,</i>

00:40:02.150 --> 00:40:06.362
<i>but its exact function,</i>
<i>like so much about narwhals,</i>

00:40:06.696 --> 00:40:08.948
<i>is still, in part, a mystery.</i>

00:40:14.662 --> 00:40:18.374
<i>Each summer, narwhals travel</i>
<i>to the same part of the Arctic,</i>

00:40:18.750 --> 00:40:23.129
<i>and patiently wait at the ice edge</i>
<i>for a crack to open up.</i>

00:40:26.174 --> 00:40:30.136
<i>A lead in the ice,</i>
<i>wide enough for them to swim along.</i>

00:40:43.441 --> 00:40:45.568
<i>Eventually, a crack appears.</i>

00:40:47.904 --> 00:40:50.907
<i>Their passage to sheltered, shallow bays.</i>

00:41:12.261 --> 00:41:16.849
<i>Narwhals are extremely timid</i>
<i>and rarely filmed underwater.</i>

00:41:44.836 --> 00:41:49.340
<i>These ice whales eagerly travel</i>
<i>down the leads as soon as they open</i>

00:41:49.549 --> 00:41:52.719
<i>because they're safer from predators</i>
<i>protected by the ice.</i>

00:42:01.561 --> 00:42:05.440
<i>The leads may also give them access</i>
<i>to new feeding grounds.</i>

00:42:18.786 --> 00:42:20.580
<i>Sea ice is not just crucial</i>

00:42:20.663 --> 00:42:23.708
<i>to the lives of those creatures</i>
<i>that live around the poles.</i>

00:42:25.710 --> 00:42:30.381
<i>It plays a vital role in determining</i>
<i>the climate of the whole planet.</i>

00:42:33.551 --> 00:42:36.137
<i>A white surface keeps the earth cool</i>

00:42:36.387 --> 00:42:40.308
<i>by reflecting most of the sun's energy</i>
<i>back into space.</i>

00:42:50.693 --> 00:42:52.904
<i>A dark surface does the opposite,</i>

00:42:53.905 --> 00:42:56.824
<i>absorbing over 90 percent</i>
<i>of the sun's energy</i>

00:42:57.200 --> 00:42:58.826
<i>and so warming the earth.</i>

00:43:03.372 --> 00:43:04.707
<i>As the sea ice shrinks,</i>

00:43:04.999 --> 00:43:09.796
<i>we're starting to lose one of our planet's</i>
<i>protective white shields.</i>

00:43:13.966 --> 00:43:15.384
<i>For thousands of years,</i>

00:43:15.593 --> 00:43:19.430
<i>there's been a balance</i>
<i>in the advance and retreat of the sea ice,</i>

00:43:20.181 --> 00:43:21.808
<i>but that is no longer the case.</i>

00:43:22.558 --> 00:43:24.685
<i>Today, during the summer months,</i>

00:43:24.936 --> 00:43:30.066
<i>there is 40 percent less sea ice cover</i>
<i>than there was in 1980.</i>

00:43:39.534 --> 00:43:44.288
<i>The Arctic is warming twice as fast</i>
<i>as anywhere else on our planet.</i>

00:43:45.248 --> 00:43:50.211
<i>By 2040, the ocean here</i>
<i>will be largely free of ice</i>

00:43:50.419 --> 00:43:51.754
<i>during the summer months.</i>

00:44:09.230 --> 00:44:14.193
<i>This loss of ice will inevitably</i>
<i>have devastating consequences</i>

00:44:14.527 --> 00:44:16.737
<i>for all those that still depend on it.</i>

00:44:21.159 --> 00:44:23.369
[birds cawing]

00:44:30.168 --> 00:44:33.504
<i>The far northeastern coast </i>
<i>of Russia.</i>

00:44:36.340 --> 00:44:38.342
[grunting]

00:44:50.438 --> 00:44:55.651
<i>This is the largest gathering </i>
<i>of walrus on the planet.</i>

00:45:06.913 --> 00:45:12.251
<i>Over a hundred thousand have hauled out</i>
<i>on one single beach.</i>

00:45:19.926 --> 00:45:23.846
<i>They do so out of desperation,</i>
<i>not out of choice.</i>

00:45:27.767 --> 00:45:31.187
<i>Their natural home is out on the sea ice.</i>

00:45:38.152 --> 00:45:41.072
<i>But the ice has retreated</i>
<i>away to the north,</i>

00:45:42.740 --> 00:45:46.369
<i>and this is the closest place</i>
<i>to their feeding grounds,</i>

00:45:46.619 --> 00:45:48.037
<i>where they can find rest.</i>

00:46:02.885 --> 00:46:05.930
<i>This calf must stay close to its mother.</i>

00:46:14.522 --> 00:46:17.525
<i>But every square inch is occupied.</i>

00:46:27.159 --> 00:46:29.078
[grunting]

00:46:37.086 --> 00:46:39.880
<i>Climbing over </i>
<i>the tightly packed bodies</i>

00:46:39.964 --> 00:46:42.383
<i>is the only way across the crowd.</i>

00:46:44.969 --> 00:46:47.430
<i>Those beneath can get crushed to death.</i>

00:46:55.896 --> 00:46:58.399
<i>A stampede can occur out of nowhere.</i>

00:47:20.421 --> 00:47:25.718
<i>Under these conditions,</i>
<i>walruses are a danger to themselves.</i>

00:47:31.557 --> 00:47:35.436
<i>Some manage to find space</i>
<i>away from the crowds.</i>

00:47:40.941 --> 00:47:43.652
<i>They struggle up the 80-meter cliffs,</i>

00:47:49.533 --> 00:47:54.789
<i>an extraordinary challenge</i>
<i>for a one-tonne animal used to sea ice.</i>

00:48:08.135 --> 00:48:10.930
<i>At least up here there's space to rest.</i>

00:48:18.729 --> 00:48:21.524
<i>A walrus's eyesight out of water is poor,</i>

00:48:23.609 --> 00:48:26.153
<i>but they can sense the others down below.</i>

00:48:31.742 --> 00:48:35.454
<i>As they get hungry,</i>
<i>they need to return to the sea.</i>

00:49:22.084 --> 00:49:24.253
<i>In their desperation to do so,</i>

00:49:24.837 --> 00:49:28.841
<i>hundreds fall from heights</i>
<i>they should never have scaled.</i>

00:50:30.903 --> 00:50:36.617
<i>These mass gatherings of walrus</i>
<i>are now happening almost every year.</i>

00:50:43.791 --> 00:50:49.421
<i>So the lives of walruses,</i>
<i>like those of polar bears and seals,</i>

00:50:49.797 --> 00:50:50.714
<i>are changing.</i>

00:50:53.217 --> 00:50:56.637
<i>All are living at the frontier</i>
<i>of climate change,</i>

00:50:58.722 --> 00:51:01.392
<i>and all are suffering as a consequence.</i>

00:51:20.035 --> 00:51:26.333
<i>For now, the Arctic winter returns</i>
<i>and the sea ice reforms.</i>

00:51:28.544 --> 00:51:30.671
<i>Order is restored.</i>

00:51:34.800 --> 00:51:39.263
<i>Relief for the many creatures</i>
<i>that depend on the ice.</i>

00:51:52.568 --> 00:51:54.278
<i>But for how much longer</i>

00:51:54.653 --> 00:52:00.200
<i>will their frozen worlds</i>
<i>be a part of life on our planet?</i>

00:52:18.385 --> 00:52:20.471
<i>Please visit ourplanet.com</i>

00:52:20.763 --> 00:52:25.184
<i>to discover what we need to do now</i>
<i>to keep the polar wilderness.</i>

00:52:27.728 --> 00:52:33.567
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world</i>
<i>Singing together ♪</i>

00:52:36.278 --> 00:52:42.117
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world</i>
<i>Say it's now or never ♪</i>

00:52:44.953 --> 00:52:49.333
<i>♪ 'Cause it's not too late</i>
<i>If we change our ways ♪</i>

00:52:49.416 --> 00:52:52.920
<i>♪ And connect the dots to our problems ♪</i>

00:52:53.253 --> 00:52:59.009
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world</i>
<i>Say we're in this together ♪</i>

00:52:59.426 --> 00:53:01.095
<i>♪ We're in this together ♪</i>